.\" Man page for ax25tcpd
.\"
.TH AX25TCPD 8 "11 Aug 2026" "ax25-apps" "Linux System Administration"
.SH NAME
ax25tcpd \- interactive AX.25 frontend to an AGWPE multiplexer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ax25tcpd
.RB [ \-c
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-C
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-f ]
.RB [ \-d ]
.RB [ \-\-verbose ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ax25tcpd
is an interactive frontend to an
.BR ax25netd (8)
loop port.  Each client connection is a session: the first line is a
command, the bytes after it are packet data.  It is useful for scripts
and for talking to AX.25 services by hand.
.PP
It runs without a configuration file: the back side is then the default
loop port of
.BR ax25common.conf (5)
(the unix socket /var/run/ax25/sockets/ax25netd.sock), and the front side
is one tcp listener on
.BR localhost:8202 .
.B localhost
covers both the IPv4 and the IPv6 loopback.
.PP
At startup the back side is asked for its version.  A server that does not
answer is not an
.BR ax25netd
and is reported, rather than leaving every connect pending until it times
out.
.SH MODES
A connection is in one of two modes, and the client chooses:
.TP
.B ascii
Line oriented, and the default.  Each input line is one packet with its end
of line normalised to CR, the packet radio end of line.  Messages from this
daemon end with LF.  This is what a person at a terminal wants and what a
.BR telnet (1)
client speaks.
.TP
.B binary
An 8 bit clean byte stream.  Remote data is passed through unchanged and a
0xFF in a payload is a 0xFF.  An unconnected datagram is one packet: the
whole remaining stream, split into
.B mtu
sized chunks and delivered when the connection closes.
.PP
The mode is set by a line of its own before the command,
.B binary
or
.BR ascii ,
the way
.BR wampes (1)
asks for it, so that a script sending a binary blob has said so before the
first byte of it rather than after.  It belongs to the connection and not to
one command: a script which has said
.B binary
once may connect as often as it likes, and a person who has said
.B binary
gets binary again until they say
.BR ascii .
.PP
A unix socket is
.B ascii
unless the client says otherwise.  Nothing about the transport decides it.
.SH TELNET
TELNET is recognised, not offered.  There is no server side IAC on connect,
because that would put three bytes of protocol in front of the first packet
of every binary client, and a client that has not asked to speak TELNET has
not asked to be sent any.
.PP
Instead the first 0xFF in an
.B ascii
connection is taken for an IAC and the connection speaks TELNET from there
on.  With a server that says nothing,
.BR telnet (1)
is quiet until the interrupt key is pressed, and that is the byte that
arrives first: IAC IP rather than a 0x03.  So the detection lands on the
^C that reaches the remote as a real 0x03, which is the only thing a telnet
client needs from us.  A 0xFF in the payload is escaped on the way out as
IAC IAC, so nothing is lost to the protocol.
.PP
The byte is looked for in a session and in the order the stream is in, not
by scanning whatever one read() returned.  A command line cannot turn a
connection into TELNET: no command carries a 0xFF, and the only byte a
telnet client sends before there is a session is the interrupt key, which
at the prompt means nothing.
.PP
A
.B binary
connection never looks for it, which is what makes it 8 bit clean.  Once a
connection has been recognised as TELNET,
.B binary
is refused for the rest of that connection, because a stream we would now
eat bytes from has already been declared TELNET.  Open a new connection to
get a binary one.
.SH FRAMING
How a session is framed follows from the mode the client asked for, not from
a port number.  There is no "binary port" and no "text port" to remember, and
one listener serves both.
.PP
A connected session
.RB ( connect )
is a byte stream in both modes, so that control characters such as ^C reach
the remote at once.  In
.B ascii
a CRLF or a bare LF in the outgoing stream becomes a single CR, and a CRLF
which the client split across two of its own writes is still one CR rather
than two, so no blank line appears on the far side that was never typed.  In
.B binary
nothing is translated.  Data from the remote is passed through unchanged in
.B binary
and has its CR turned into LF in
.BR ascii .
.SH COMMANDS
Unambiguous prefixes of the commands are accepted.  The end of a session is
signalled by closing the connection, or by the
.B quit
or
.B bye
commands.
.TP
.B ascii
.BR binary
Select the mode, on a line of its own.  Both are refused while the connection
is busy with a command, and
.B binary
is refused once the connection speaks TELNET (see
.BR TELNET ).
.TP
.I connect " [" options "]" " [" port "[" / chan "]" ":" "]" dest "[" , digi , ... "]" " [" < SRC" ]"
Start an AX.25 connection.  The port is a name,
optionally with a channel number after a
.BR / ,
or a flat port number;
.B loop
names the virtual loopback port.  A channel has to be a number from 0 to 15;
anything else is an error rather than channel 0.  Commas and spaces both
separate a path and mix freely, so
.I "DB0AAA-8 DB0BBB"
means the same as
.IR DB0AAA-8,DB0BBB .
.PP
The port and the destination are one argument,
.IR "hf/2:DB0AAA" ,
because two arguments cannot say which of them is which.  With an
autorouter on the far side,
.B "connect hf DB0AAA"
reads equally well as port
.B hf
to
.BR DB0AAA ,
and as a call to
.B hf
digipeated by
.BR DB0AAA .
The colon settles it: the argument is split on its first colon, always,
since a callsign has no colon in it.  The old two argument form is refused
with a message that quotes the argument as it was typed, so that it can be
pasted back after fixing the one character it is about.
.PP
Without a port the
.B default-port
from
.BR ax25tcpd.conf (5)
is used, and the netd resolves a digipeater path itself.  With neither the
command is refused.  While connecting, input is held and delivered once the
connection is up, through the same path as everything that follows, so the
first packet of a session is framed like the second one even when a script
wrote command and payload together.  A connect the netd does not answer
within ten minutes is given up.
.TP
.I datagram " [" options "]" " [" port "[" / chan "]" ":" "]" " [" dest "[" , digi , ... "]" "]" " [" < SRC" ]"
Send unconnected (UI) frames.  In
.B ascii
every line is one frame.  In
.B binary
the whole remaining stream is one datagram, accumulated and sent when the
connection closes.
.PP
With a
.I dest
the header is fixed for the whole session and every line is payload.
Without one, every line is a whole TNC2 frame
.RB ( "SRC>DEST,DIGI,...:payload" )
whose source and path come out of the line itself.  Like
.BR connect ,
it needs a port, from the argument or from
.BR default-port .
.TP
.B quit
.BR bye
Close the session.
.TP
.B help
List the commands.
.SH PORT NAMES
A port name is looked up in three places, in this order, and the first that
claims it wins:
.TP
.B the netd's own port list
The names of the upstreams of the
.BR ax25netd (8)
this daemon is connected to, as it reported them.  This comes first because it
is the server that will carry the frame, and its numbering is the one that
counts; a
.B "target tcp"
or
.B "target socket"
line pointing at another machine would make any other answer wrong.
.TP
.B axports
The names in
.BR axports (5),
which the netd's list does not carry \(em that list holds upstream names, and
a port is called by whatever name the local file gives it.  So
.B agwpe-direwolf
is a port here even though the netd only knows
.BR direwolf ,
and it is the same port:
.B "call agwpe-direwolf"
and
.B "connect agwpe-direwolf:DB0AAA"
now mean the same thing.
.B loop
and
.B agwpe-loop
are the virtual loopback either way.
.TP
.B a flat port number
0 to 255, for a script that knows the answer.  Always worked, still does.
.PP
A name that is a kernel AX.25 interface or a port of a WAMPES node is refused
by name, with the reason, because this daemon sends through
.BR ax25netd (8)
and has no other way out:
.B "connect --port bpq0 DB0AAA"
is answered with
.B "it is a kernel AX.25 port, and this daemon sends through ax25netd only".
A refusal that says "no such port" would be a false statement about a port
that does exist and that other programs use \(em the name is taken here for
one thing and nowhere else.
.PP
A WAMPES name contains a colon, and the first colon of a
.I port:dest
argument is the boundary, so such a name can only be given whole, through
.BR \-\-port :
.B "connect --port wampes:hfb DB0AAA".
.PP
.BR ax25netctl (8)
answers what a name is and whether the netd agrees, which is the question to
ask first when a connect of this daemon does not go out.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-silent
Suppress the "*** CONNECTED" and "*** DISCONNECTED" status lines.
.TP
.B \-\-keep
Do not end the session when a connection closes; the command prompt
returns and a new connect or datagram may be issued.
.TP
.BI \-\-pid= XX
Set the protocol identifier.  Accepts
.BR text ,
.BR netrom ,
.BR rose ,
.BR flexnet ,
.BR l3
or a number.  The default is text (0xF0).
.TP
.BI \-\-port " port"
Give the port separately, as an alternative spelling of the
.I port:dest
form, for a script which keeps the port in a variable and finds the colon
awkward.  A channel after a
.B /
is accepted here too.
.TP
.BI \-\-mycall " call"
Give the source call, exactly like the
.B < SRC
argument.
.SH SOURCE CALL
The source call comes from the optional
.B < SRC
argument, from
.B \-\-mycall
or from the
.B default-call
directive in
.BR ax25tcpd.conf (5).
Without either, the command is refused.
.SH GENERAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c " file"
Use
.I file
instead of the default configuration file.  A missing file is not an
error and leaves the defaults in place (see DESCRIPTION).
.TP
.BI \-C " file"
Use
.I file
as the loop port configuration instead of the default
\fI@@@AX25_SYSCONFDIR@@@/ax25common.conf\fR.
The loop port is the endpoint
.BR ax25netd (8)
listens on; the back side of this instance is taken from that file (see
.BR ax25common.conf (5))
unless the
.B target
directive in
.BR ax25tcpd.conf (5)
is set.
.TP
.B \-f
Stay in the foreground.
.TP
.B \-d
Log to standard error.
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
Say the decisions the daemon makes: a line when a client connects,
when a command names a port and a destination, and when a session comes
up or goes down.  Repeatable, like the other daemons'; the second one is
the
.B \-d
trace.
.SH FILES
.I @@@AX25_SYSCONFDIR@@@/ax25tcpd.conf
.br
.I @@@AX25_SYSCONFDIR@@@/ax25common.conf
(back side unless overridden)
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ax25tcpd.conf (5),
.BR ax25netd (8),
.BR ax25netctl (8),
.BR axports (5),
.BR ax25common.conf (5),
.BR ax25netd_agwpe.conf (5),
.BR wampes (1)